title: Trace Gradient Paths
keyword: FLUXPRINT
manual: gradientpath/#c2-fluxprint

Traces gradient paths of a scalar field, the curves that follow its gradient up to a
maximum (usually a nucleus) and down to a minimum, and draws them as tubes in the 3D view.
Paths started around the critical points draw the elements of the
QTAIM topology (interatomic surfaces, ring surfaces and ring paths), as FLUXPRINT does.
Choose the "Field" at the top; it needs critical points, found with the Critical Points
window or read from a checkpoint.

# Seeds

- "Single shot": "Pick" traces the paths up and down from a point clicked in the view (an
  atom gives its position; empty space, the clicked point).
- "Symmetry-equivalent paths" (crystals): trace the paths of the selected symmetry-unique
  CPs once and carry them to all their copies in the cell; otherwise the table lists every
  CP in the cell and only the selected ones are traced.
- The table lists the CPs of the field. "All", "None" and "Toggle" change the selection and
  "Pick" adds a CP clicked in the view. Without CPs beyond the nuclei, "Calculate Critical
  Points" opens the Critical Points window.

# Paths

- "Color" and "Radius (Å)": color and tube radius of the new paths.
- More sets the paths per kind of CP. NCP and CCP: directions spread over a sphere
  ("Azimuthal" and "Polar" counts). BCP: paths down in the plane perpendicular to the bond
  path, the interatomic surface ("Number" of paths; "Angles" picks the starting directions:
  uniform, quotient of the Hessian eigenvalues, or dynamical). RCP: the two ring paths down
  the axis ("Ring path") and paths up in the perpendicular plane, the ring surface. The bond
  paths themselves are already traced by the CP search.
- "Trace" adds the paths of the selected CPs to the gradient paths object of the field in
  the view (created if there is none), one group per CP. "Estimate Cost" times a sample of
  them. "Edit Gradient Paths" opens that object.

# Notes

- "Clear" removes all the traced paths from the object; the bond paths stay.
